WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.792 --> 00:00:15.333
"I've got people in me."

00:00:15.375 --> 00:00:18.226
So sang the late Abbey Lincoln.

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:21.226
I take that lyric as mantra.

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:23.292
"I've got people in me."

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:29.726
Jomama Jones is the person in me
I turn to as a guide.

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:31.768
She's my alter ego.

00:00:31.792 --> 00:00:35.768
I've been embodying her
in performance since 1995,

00:00:35.792 --> 00:00:41.601
and she comes around
when she has some insight to offer folks.

00:00:41.625 --> 00:00:43.684
At this time of radical change,

00:00:43.708 --> 00:00:47.917
I'm glad to be the vessel
for her message to you.

00:00:53.542 --> 00:00:55.143
Jomama Jones: What if I told you

00:00:55.167 --> 00:00:57.559
it's going to be alright ...

00:00:57.583 --> 00:00:59.583
but what if I told you not yet?

00:01:00.542 --> 00:01:03.143
What if I told you there are trials ahead

00:01:03.167 --> 00:01:05.976
beyond your deepest fears?

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.893
What if I told you will you fall ...

00:01:08.917 --> 00:01:11.125
down, down, down?

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.518
But what if I told you
you will surprise yourself?

00:01:17.542 --> 00:01:22.000
What if I told you will be brave enough?

00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:25.184
What if I told you

00:01:25.208 --> 00:01:27.417
we won't all make it through?

00:01:28.333 --> 00:01:30.643
But what if I told you

00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:34.059
that is as it must be?

00:01:34.083 --> 00:01:38.417
What if I told you I've seen the future?

00:01:41.125 --> 00:01:43.184
Do you like my hands?

00:01:43.208 --> 00:01:45.518
They're expressive, yeah?

00:01:45.542 --> 00:01:48.018
Now look at your hands -- now go on.

00:01:48.042 --> 00:01:50.726
There's so much history recorded
through their touches

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:55.309
and marks of the future
sketched on their palms.

00:01:55.333 --> 00:01:57.143
Sometimes hands grip tight,

00:01:57.167 --> 00:01:59.958
sometimes hands let go.

00:02:01.375 --> 00:02:03.768
What if I told you

00:02:03.792 --> 00:02:06.292
it's all going to come undone?

00:02:07.083 --> 00:02:08.476
Hm.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:10.226
Ladies and gentlemen

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:12.768
and otherwise described,

00:02:12.792 --> 00:02:15.893
I am Jomama Jones.

00:02:15.917 --> 00:02:18.768
Some call me a soul sonic superstar,

00:02:18.792 --> 00:02:20.684
and I agree,

00:02:20.708 --> 00:02:24.143
though even in my past
that was from the future.

00:02:24.167 --> 00:02:26.268
Let me take you back to girlhood.

00:02:26.292 --> 00:02:27.851
Picture this:

00:02:27.875 --> 00:02:29.726
it was Planting Day,

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:31.893
which was a holiday I invented

00:02:31.917 --> 00:02:34.476
for the Black youth
community group I founded.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.601
I dashed home to put on
my gardening ensemble

00:02:37.625 --> 00:02:41.643
when I caught my uncle Freeman red-handed.

00:02:41.667 --> 00:02:46.684
He was standing over my piggy bank
with his hammer raised high.

00:02:46.708 --> 00:02:48.958
He was fixing to steal my coins.

00:02:49.667 --> 00:02:50.976
And you see,

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.476
my uncle Freeman was a handyman.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:54.851
He could fix anything --

00:02:54.875 --> 00:02:57.059
a broken chair, a shattered pot --

00:02:57.083 --> 00:03:00.351
even bring grandmother's
plants back to life.

00:03:00.375 --> 00:03:04.476
He had that magic touch
with broken things ...

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:06.184
and broken people.

00:03:06.208 --> 00:03:08.184
He would take me with him on his jobs

00:03:08.208 --> 00:03:09.518
and say, "C'mon Jo,

00:03:09.542 --> 00:03:12.893
let's go do something
to make this world a better place."

00:03:12.917 --> 00:03:15.143
His hands were wide and calloused,

00:03:15.167 --> 00:03:20.643
and they always reminded me
of displaced tree roots.

00:03:20.667 --> 00:03:24.018
As we worked he would talk with folks

00:03:24.042 --> 00:03:27.976
about the change he was sure
was just around the corner.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.351
I saw him mend flagging hopes

00:03:32.375 --> 00:03:35.101
and leave folks
with their heads held high.

00:03:35.125 --> 00:03:38.042
His hands stirred the sunshine.

00:03:38.875 --> 00:03:43.018
And now he was about
to break my piggy bank.

00:03:43.042 --> 00:03:46.143
I said "Step back, man,
and show me your hands."

00:03:46.167 --> 00:03:48.601
You know the irony was

00:03:48.625 --> 00:03:54.393
he used to give me all the old coins
he'd find under floorboards while working.

00:03:54.417 --> 00:03:56.018
And I put them in the piggy bank

00:03:56.042 --> 00:03:59.875
along with the money I earned
through my childhood side hustles.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.976
But by the spring of 1970,

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:06.851
Uncle Freeman had lost his touch ...

00:04:06.875 --> 00:04:09.500
along with most of his jobs.

00:04:10.333 --> 00:04:13.809
He saw a heavy future

00:04:13.833 --> 00:04:19.643
of civil wrongs and Black power
outages in his palms.

00:04:19.667 --> 00:04:22.768
The last straw had come
the previous winter

00:04:22.792 --> 00:04:26.333
when they had gunned down Fred Hampton.

00:04:27.417 --> 00:04:30.434
Overwhelmed with fear

00:04:30.458 --> 00:04:32.226
and rage

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:33.559
and grief,

00:04:33.583 --> 00:04:37.018
Uncle Freeman tried to game his future.

00:04:37.042 --> 00:04:38.976
He gripped too tight,

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.309
and he started playing the numbers.

00:04:41.333 --> 00:04:43.934
"Well, one of these numbers
is gonna hit, little girl.

00:04:43.958 --> 00:04:46.018
You got a quarter for your uncle Free -- "

00:04:46.042 --> 00:04:48.518
Now some of y'all have that relative.

00:04:48.542 --> 00:04:52.101
But I knew right then and there
I had to do something.

00:04:52.125 --> 00:04:53.934
I jumped up and I grabbed that hammer

00:04:53.958 --> 00:04:56.184
and I brought it
crashing down on that pig.

00:04:56.208 --> 00:05:00.309
And Uncle Freeman started to weep
as I gathered up all the coins.

00:05:00.333 --> 00:05:02.851
"We're not buying
no lottery ticket, Uncle Freeman.

00:05:02.875 --> 00:05:04.125
C'mon."

00:05:05.333 --> 00:05:10.583
We spent every last cent
at the seed store.

00:05:11.542 --> 00:05:13.559
You know, the kids in my gardening group?

00:05:13.583 --> 00:05:16.226
They didn't bat an eye
when I had Uncle Freeman get down

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:18.684
and put his hands in the earth again

00:05:18.708 --> 00:05:21.726
and start breaking up
that soil for our seeds.

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:25.559
And my little friend Taesha even came over
and started slapping him on the back

00:05:25.583 --> 00:05:27.309
saying, "Cry it out, Uncle Freeman.

00:05:27.333 --> 00:05:28.583
Cry it out."

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.292
"I can't fix this," he sobbed.

00:05:34.583 --> 00:05:38.125
It's an ancient-future truism, that.

00:05:39.042 --> 00:05:42.625
He wasn't the first to feel that way,
and he wouldn't be the last.

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:49.500
Right now, it feels as though
everything is breaking beyond repair.

00:05:50.625 --> 00:05:51.875
It is.

00:05:52.708 --> 00:05:57.684
But that breaking apart
can be a breaking open,

00:05:57.708 --> 00:05:59.809
no matter how violent and uncertain

00:05:59.833 --> 00:06:02.125
and fearsome it seems.

00:06:03.125 --> 00:06:05.393
The thing is ...

00:06:05.417 --> 00:06:07.458
we can't do it alone.

00:06:08.958 --> 00:06:14.643
Uncle Freeman cried so much that day
as we planted our seeds,

00:06:14.667 --> 00:06:17.667
he was our very own irrigation system.

00:06:20.125 --> 00:06:22.476
"I don't know who I am
anymore, little girl,"

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:24.833
he said to me at sundown.

00:06:26.083 --> 00:06:28.643
"Good, Uncle Freeman.

00:06:28.667 --> 00:06:29.958
Good.

00:06:30.833 --> 00:06:33.518
You're new again,

00:06:33.542 --> 00:06:36.792
and that's just how we need you."

